---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Why The Layer Line exists, who writes it, and what it is and isn't."
  path="/about/"
>
  <div class="container prose-page">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'About', href: '/about/' }]} />
    <header class="prose-page__header">
      <h1>About The Layer Line</h1>
      <p class="prose-page__lead">
        A small, independent site about desktop FDM 3D printing, specifically the part where a print goes wrong
        and you need to know why.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-page__body">
      <p>
        The Layer Line started from a simple observation: most 3D printing content either explains what a term
        means without telling you what to do about it, or hands you a giant settings table with no explanation of
        why any particular number matters. Neither one helps much when a print just failed and you need to know
        whether it's a temperature problem, a mechanical problem, or something else entirely.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site tries to sit in the middle: explain the actual mechanism behind a defect (why stringing happens,
        why corners warp, why layer lines show up where they do), then give a specific, ordered set of things to
        check. The goal is that after reading a guide once, you can diagnose the same category of problem yourself
        next time, without coming back.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site is not</h2>
      <p>
        It's not a printer review site, and it's not run by a printer or filament manufacturer. Guides here are
        based on well-documented engineering principles of FDM printing (thermoplastics, extrusion, motion
        control), not on hands-on testing of specific printer models. Where a guide makes a claim that depends on a
        specific product or manufacturer's documentation, it links to that source directly rather than presenting
        it as our own testing.
      </p>

      <h2>How the site is run</h2>
      <p>
        Content is planned and written with AI-assisted research and drafting tools, then edited for accuracy and
        clarity. See the <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> for the specifics of how that works and
        what it doesn't cover. The site earns revenue through Google AdSense; see
        <a href="/privacy-policy/">the privacy policy</a> for how that affects data collection.
      </p>

      <h2>Get in touch</h2>
      <p>
        Found something inaccurate, outdated, or unclear? That's useful to know. See the
        <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> for how to reach us.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
